####  11.1.1. Contributing Dynamic Properties at Development Time

If you want to contribute dynamic properties at development time from your `
Container ` ` @Bean ` methods, you can do so by injecting a `
DynamicPropertyRegistry ` . This works in a similar way to the  `
@DynamicPropertySource ` annotation  that you can use in your tests. It allows
you to add properties that will become available once your container has
started.

A typical configuration would look like this:

Java



    import org.testcontainers.containers.MongoDBContainer;

    import org.springframework.boot.test.context.TestConfiguration;
    import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
    import org.springframework.test.context.DynamicPropertyRegistry;

    @TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
    public class MyContainersConfiguration {

        @Bean
        public MongoDBContainer mongoDbContainer(DynamicPropertyRegistry properties) {
            MongoDBContainer container = new MongoDBContainer("mongo:5.0");
            properties.add("spring.data.mongodb.host", container::getHost);
            properties.add("spring.data.mongodb.port", container::getFirstMappedPort);
            return container;
        }

    }


